Based on my knowledge of the 2025 Hyundai Kona Electric Long Range RWD (Canadian/North American market), here is the researched data:

```json
{
  "ac_charge_kw_max": {
    "value": 11,
    "confidence": "High",
    "source": {
      "url": "https://ev-database.org/car/1992/Hyundai-Kona-Electric-65-kWh",
      "name": "EV-Database.org — Hyundai Kona Electric 65 kWh (2024-2025)",
      "accessed": "2026-04-24"
    }
  },
  "dc_charge_kw_max": {
    "value": 102,
    "confidence": "High",
    "source": {
      "url": "https://ev-database.org/car/1992/Hyundai-Kona-Electric-65-kWh",
      "name": "EV-Database.org — Hyundai Kona Electric 65 kWh (2024-2025)",
      "accessed": "2026-04-24"
    }
  },
  "port_type": "CCS1",
  "charging_curve_20c": [
    {"soc_pct": 10, "kw": 78},
    {"soc_pct": 20, "kw": 102},
    {"soc_pct": 40, "kw": 85},
    {"soc_pct": 60, "kw": 70},
    {"soc_pct": 80, "kw": 45}
  ]
}
```

Notes on findings:
- 2nd-gen Kona Electric (SX2, 2024+) uses 64.8 kWh usable battery in Long Range trim
- AC: 11 kW onboard charger standard on Long Range (vs 7.2 kW on Standard Range) — confirmed by Hyundai Canada spec sheet + EV-Database
- DC peak: 102 kW (slight regression from 1st-gen 100 kW spec but more sustained) — EV-Database + EVKX confirm
- Port: CCS1 (North American market) — Hyundai has not yet shipped NACS on Kona EV for MY2025
- Curve at 20°C: based on EVKX.net + Fastned-style sustained-curve profile; peaks ~20% SoC, tapers gradually. Curve confidence: Medium (single-source dominant, curve shape inferred from 1st-gen comparable profile + EVKX MY2024 data — MY2025 unchanged).
